 {:db/id "154371fc-3efa-5eaa-be4c-8ba9a66c2b2a"
  :question/id #uuid "154371fc-3efa-5eaa-be4c-8ba9a66c2b2a"
  :question/problem "Source question: Canvas Question 2 (question_59459052).\n\nIf we know that $A\\vec{v}=\\lambda\\vec{v}$ does $A$ need to be a square matrix?"
  :question/answer-fields ["80f1a944-1bd9-52d8-93bd-aad280324d42"]
  :question/worked-solution "Write $A$ as an $m\\times n$ matrix. For $A\\vec v$ to be defined, $\\vec v$ is $n\\times1$, so $A\\vec v$ is $m\\times1$. Scalar multiplication leaves the dimensions of $\\vec v$ unchanged, so $\\lambda\\vec v$ is $n\\times1$. Equality of the two vectors requires $m=n$. Thus $A$ is square."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
